These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Light through surfaces {#c8 number=8 target=355 summary="Transmission in the Enhanced and Skin lighting models (thick objects after Barré-Brisebois and Bouchard, thin ones after Jimenez), the shadow-map thickness it reads and its units, what a leaf is in O3DE, screen-space subsurface scattering, and the Skin and Eye models." sources="skin-lighting-defines, sss-sample-count, transmission-option, back-lighting, transmission-params, transmission-kernel, shadow-thickness, shadow-thickness-camera-span, transmission-finalize, foliage-sample-material, double-sided-cull, back-face-normal, vertex-normal-unflipped, sss-pack, sss-kernel, skin-diffuse, eye-direct-lighting"}

### Only Enhanced and Skin let light through {#c8-which kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.5 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 48.0 4.0 2.0} {pose 40.0 8.0 1.7}}}}}}}

Light that passes through a surface is called transmission.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 30.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "transmission-option"}}}
In O3DE, transmission is compiled only into the Enhanced and Skin lighting models.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 10.0 sentence}}}
StandardPBR, the default, has none.

${overCallout
    {' {teapotTransmissionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "transmission-params"
    "In an EnhancedPBR material, transmission is off by default, in the mode None."}

${cue "key" {' {teapotTransmission 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}
The two other modes are thick object and thin object.

### Thick objects: light from behind, scattered toward the eye {#c8-thick kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' -20.0} {' 30.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingTransmission {highlightCode "transmission" {' "thick"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "back-lighting"}}}
The thick object mode, after Barré-Brisebois and Bouchard, brightens where the eye looks toward the light through the object.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionPowerRange 2.0 16.0}}
    "back-lighting"
    "The model measures how directly the eye looks toward the light, with the light's direction bent a little by the normal."}

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionPower 10.0 sentence}}}
That alignment is raised to a power.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionPower 16.0 sentence}}}
A higher power gathers the glow more tightly toward the light's direction.

${cue "key" {' {transmissionPower 6.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${overCallout
    {' {transmissionThicknessRange 0.05 0.9}}
    "back-lighting"
    "The glow fades as the object gets thicker, by the exponential of minus four times the thickness."}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunPitch 30.0 sentence}}}
The thickness is the depth of the object between the point and the sun, which a later beat measures.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionThickness 1.0 sentence}}}
The glow is also multiplied by one minus the thickness, so it reaches zero at a thickness of one.

### Thin objects: the back face's light, through a kernel {#c8-thin kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.4 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}
    {transmissionThickness 0.5}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunPitch} {' {' 10.0} {' 22.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' panelCode listingTransmission {highlightCode "transmission" {' "thin"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "back-lighting"}}}
The thin model, after Jimenez, starts from the light falling on the object's far side, its back face.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 30.0 sentence}}}
That light is the albedo times the back face's N dot L.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionThicknessRange 0.1 1.0}}
    "transmission-kernel"
    "The thin model then weakens that light by a falloff that depends on the measured thickness and on the scatter distance, how far light travels inside the material."}

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionThickness 0.8 sentence}}}
The scatter distance is set per colour channel.

${cue "key" {' {transmissionThickness 0.5} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "transmission-params"}}}
The default scatter distance is eight for red, two point one six for green, and one point zero four for blue.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionThickness 0.9 sentence}}}
So red passes much farther than blue, and the glow turns orange.

${overCallout
    {' {sunYawRange 10.0 60.0}}
    "back-lighting"
    "As the sun moves from behind the teapot to its side, the back face turns away from it and the glow fades."}

### Thickness from the shadow map, and its units {#c8-thickness kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.3 {'
    {normalShadowBias 2.5}
    {transmissionThickness 0.5}
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath transmissionSweep}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunPitch 30.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {inset "cm"}
    imageFromCascadeMap
    {imageCascade 4.0}
    {imageParamFrom 0.9}
    imageCornerBottomLeft
    {imageTitle "views-cascade_map"}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness"}}}
The thickness comes from the sun's cascade.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunPitch 40.0 sentence}}}
It is the depth between the shaded point and the first surface the sun meets.

${cue "key" {pickTo atP objTeapot}}
In the cascade view, the ring marks the teapot's point.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 30.0 sentence}}}
The depth stored there belongs to the side facing the sun, and the point's own depth is the far side.

${cue "key" {' {glide shadowFar 30.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness-camera-span"}}}
The code shows that this difference is measured as a share of the cascade's own depth range.

${cue "key" {' {glide shadowFar 100.0 sentence}}}
But the code turns that share into a distance with the camera's depth span instead, so the result is not in metres.

${cue "key" {' {glide normalShadowBias 0.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "shadow-thickness"}}}
When the thickness of a flat, thin surface like a leaf is measured, its point is shifted twice.

${cue "key" {' {normalShadowBias 2.5} {blend 1.5}}}
With the default biases, the normal offset of chapter five and the thin mode's shift into the surface nearly cancel.

So the measured thickness is about zero, and the glow runs at full strength.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionScale 0.0 sentence}}}
Last comes where the glow is added.

${overCallout
    {' {transmissionScaleRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "transmission-finalize"
    "The transmitted light is tinted, then added to the diffuse light after the material's occlusion."}

So that occlusion never darkens it.

### A leaf in O3DE is a material, not a lighting model {#c8-foliage kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelValues 0.5 {'
    {cameraYaw 110.0}
    {shadowFar 100.0}
    {normalShadowBias 2.5}
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 110.0 6.0 2.6} {pose 120.0 4.0 2.8} {pose 112.0 8.0 2.5}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 25.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "foliage-sample-material"}}} O3DE has no foliage shading of its own.

Its vegetation system places ordinary meshes, with whatever material they carry.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "cutout-clip"}}}
A plain leaf is StandardPBR, cut out by its alpha and drawn double-sided.

It stays dark when lit from behind, because Standard has no transmission.

${overCallout
    {' {teapotTransmissionRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "foliage-sample-material"
    "O3DE's own leaf-like test material is EnhancedPBR, double-sided, in thin mode with a green tint."}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 30.0 sentence}}}
The lab shows that material, in thin mode, on its teapot.

${cue "key" {' {leafNormals 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "vertex-normal-unflipped"}}}
A leaf's back face has one more catch.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 179.0}} sentence}}}}}
The shading normal, the one the lighting uses, is flipped there, but the mesh's vertex normal is not.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 112.0 sentence}}}
Transmission and the shadow offset both use that unflipped vertex normal.

### Subsurface scattering: a blur of the diffuse light {#c8-sss kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelHidden 0.5 {'
    {cameraYaw 70.0}
    {leafNormals 0.0}
    {sssScale 1.0}
    {sssProfile 0.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 60.0}
    {sunPitch 40.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 1.0}
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 70.0 6.0 2.0} {pose 66.0 5.0 2.0} {pose 74.0 8.0 1.9}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {sssProfile 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "enhanced-draw-list"}}}
Subsurface scattering spreads light under the surface, so it leaves a little away from where it entered.

${cue "key" {' {teapotTransmission 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}
Enhanced and Skin materials with scattering on write one more target, the scatter distance.

${cue "key" {chainTarget "sss" 0.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views-target_diffuse"}}}}
They also pack two settings into the diffuse target's alpha.

${cue "key" {pickTo atSphere objSphere}}
One is a scattering factor, how strong the scattering is, and the other is a quality setting.

${cue "key" {' {inset "sss"} {imageChannel 4.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-pack"}}}
The pack is the factor's byte times two hundred and fifty-six, plus the quality's byte.

${cue "key" {pickTo atP objTeapot}}
That integer is stored in a half float, which holds integers exactly only up to two thousand and forty-eight, so larger packs come back rounded.

${cue "key" {' {glide sssScale 1.5 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-kernel"}}}
A compute pass then blurs the diffuse light with Burley's normalised diffusion profile, a curve of how far light spreads under the surface.

${cue "key" {' {sssProfile 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
The lab draws that curve for each colour channel, as an illustration.

${cue "key" {' {glide sssScale 0.5 sentence}}}
The blur takes two hundred samples a pixel at full quality, and eighty at the default quality of zero point four.

${cue "key" {' {glide sssScale 2.5 sentence}}}
The blur's footprint is set by the scatter distance, in millimetres.

${cue "key" {' {glide sssScale 1.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "sss-sample-count"}}} The lab does not run this pass.

In the lab, the targets carry a packed alpha of minus one, which means no scattering, for every surface.

### Skin and Eye {#c8-skin kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDiffuse panelValues 0.4 {'
    {teapotTransmission 0.0}
    {sssProfile 0.0}
    {vectors 0.0}
    {lobe 0.0}
    viewModeFinal}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath brdfSweep}}}}}

${over
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.3 0.9}}
    {cat {'
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' diffuseModelNormalizedDisney}}
        {text " "}
        {cue "key" {' {callout "skin-diffuse"}}}
        {text
            " Skin, the other model with transmission, always uses Disney's normalised diffuse, rough or not."}}}}

It also offers a second specular lobe, blended with the first.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 40.0} {' 6.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "skin-lighting-defines"}}}
Skin takes the sky's light inside the forward pass, and uses a human skin profile for thin transmission.

${cue "key" {' diffuseModelLambertian}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {eased easingSine {orbit {' lightX lightY} {' -1.05 1.24} 60.0 sentence}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "eye-direct-lighting"}}}
Eye, the material type for eyes, uses the Skin model's material setup, but StandardPBR's direct lighting.

Its iris looks refracted, because the shader shifts the iris texture inside a sphere.

### Light that passes through {#c8-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTransmission panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {transmissionThickness 0.5}
    {labels 0.0}
    {sunLux 40.0}
    {iblExposure 1.0}
    {skyboxExposure 2.0}
    {exposure 0.35}
    {sunYaw 10.0}
    {sunPitch 22.0}
    {sphereLight 0.0}
    {walls 0.0}
    {teapotTransmission 2.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 35.0 6.0 1.8} {pose 48.0 4.0 2.0} {pose 40.0 8.0 1.7}}}
        {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 10.0}} beat}}}}}

So transmission is extra light from behind, added per light inside the forward pass, and only for Enhanced and Skin materials.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionThickness 0.8 sentence}}}
Transmission reuses the sun's shadow map for thickness.

${cue "key" {' {glide transmissionThickness 0.5 sentence}}}
That keeps transmission cheap, but it makes the thickness scale approximate.

$settle Every surface so far went through a material type.

$settle But terrain brings its own forward shader.

