These are the files and lines this chapter followed, in reading order.

## Terrain, and a word on hair {#c9 number=9 target=265 summary="The Terrain gem's own forward shader: fixed macro values, normals made per vertex on the CPU, detail materials blended by weight, multiscatter in the sky's light but not in direct light; and the Marschner model of the AtomTressFX hair gem." sources="opaque-pass-order, terrain-pass-request, terrain-forward-draw-list, terrain-macro-constants, terrain-normal-cpu, terrain-normal-ps, terrain-height-blend, terrain-height-blend-option, terrain-no-direct-multiscatter, terrain-multiscatter-flags, hair-default-model, hair-marschner, hair-longitudinal, hair-cuticle-default"}

### Terrain's own shader {#c9-own kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.8 {'
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 28.0 22.0} {pose 10.0 22.0 24.0} {pose 36.0 32.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunPitch 30.0 sentence}}}
Terrain is drawn with a forward shader of its own.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 140.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {chainTarget "ta" 2.0 imageCornerTopLeft {' {imageTitle "views-target_albedo"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-forward-draw-list"}}}
The Terrain gem's material type lists its own forward, depth, and shadow shaders.

${cue "key" {' {inset "ta"} {imageTarget 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}
They draw into the same forward pass, and into the same five targets.

${cue "key" {' {inset "ta"} {imageTarget 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}
$settle ${cue "key" {'
    {inset "ta"}
    {mark 0.0 0.18 1.0 0.92}
    {imageMarkText "views-mark_hills"}
    {blend 0.6}}} In the albedo view, the hills sit in the same target as everything else.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 18.0 sentence}}}
They show their macro colour, the coarse colour that covers the whole terrain.

$settle So the later passes read a terrain pixel exactly as they read the room's.

${cue "key" {' {inset "ta"} unmark}}
${overCallout
    {' {forwardStageRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "opaque-pass-order"
    "The sky's light, the reflections, and the occlusion likewise reach the hills through the same full-screen passes as everything else."}

${cue "key" {' {clipmapRings 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {imageHide {' "ta"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-pass-request"}}}
The terrain's feature processor, the engine part that manages it, adds only compute passes.

Those passes fill clipmaps, caches of the terrain's textures that are finest near the camera.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraPitch 45.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 30.0 sentence}}}
The lab draws those clipmaps on its hills as an illustration.

${cue "key" {' {clipmapRings 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}
These compute passes are off by default.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 170.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-forward-draw-list"}}}
The terrain casts and receives the sun's shadows like any opaque surface, and reads the same shadow buffer.

### Fixed macro values {#c9-fixed kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.5 {'
    {clipmapRings 0.0}
    {multiscatter 1.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {paramFormula 0.0}
    {sunYaw 120.0}
    {sunPitch 35.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 18.0 16.0} {pose 14.0 14.0 18.0} {pose 36.0 20.0 15.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 140.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {term 1}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-macro-constants"}}}
The macro material is the coarse one that covers the whole terrain.

${cue "key" {' viewModeSpecular}}
Where only the macro material covers the ground, the shader fixes roughness at one and metalness at zero.

${cue "key" {' {term 2}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeSpecular}}
${overCallout
    {' {sunPitchRange 35.0 8.0}}
    "terrain-macro-constants"
    "The macro material's specular factor of a half gives an F zero of zero point zero four."}

So even with the sun low, the hills keep only a broad, faint sheen.

${cue "key" {' {term 3}}}
${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal {sunPitch 35.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-no-direct-multiscatter"}}}
The terrain's lighting data never computes the multiscatter compensation of chapter four.

${cue "key" {' viewModeMultiscatter}}
So its direct light uses a compensation factor of one.

${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal}}
Even so, the multiscatter flag in the normal target's alpha, from chapter one, still asks the sky's reflection pass for compensation.

${overCallout
    {' {multiscatterRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "terrain-multiscatter-flags"
    "Turning the flag off changes only the sky's reflection on the hills."}

That is because their direct light was never compensated.

${cue "key" {' {multiscatter 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-height-blend-option"}}}
So much for the macro material.

${cue "key" {' {detailRange 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 14.0 sentence}}}
Up close, detail materials replace it, blended by plain weights.

Detail materials could also blend by height instead, but that option is off by default.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 22.0 sentence}}}
The lab's hills have no detail materials, so the ring only marks where they would apply.

### Normals made on the CPU, per vertex, in eight bits {#c9-normal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelValues 0.4 {'
    {detailRange 0.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 0.0}
    {sunYaw 120.0}
    {sunPitch 20.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 34.0 18.0} {pose 14.0 30.0 20.0} {pose 36.0 36.0 17.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunPitch 10.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-cpu"}}}
The terrain's normals are computed on the CPU, at each vertex, from central differences of the heights.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 60.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-cpu"}}}
Each axis's slope is normalised separately, by dividing it by the square root of one plus the slope squared.

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 90.0 sentence}}}
The result is stored as an eight bit signed number.

${overCallout
    {' {terrainO3deNormalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "terrain-normal-ps"
    "The vertex shader rebuilds the up component from the two stored slopes, as the square root of one minus the sum of their squares."}

${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 100.0 sentence}}}
That is exact where the ground slopes along one axis, and wrong where it slopes both ways.

${cue "key" {' viewModeTerrainNormalError}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraPitch} {' {' 60.0 40.0} {' 10.0 30.0}} sentence}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "terrain-normal-ps"}}}
This view shows the angle between O3DE's normal and the exact one on the lab's hills.

The angle is near zero on gentle ground, and many degrees on the steepest two-way slopes.

${cue "key" {' viewModeFinal}}
${cue "key" {' {glide sunYaw 150.0 sentence}}}
The terrain can have a macro normal map, a coarse normal texture for the whole terrain.

${cue "key" {' {terrainO3deNormal 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}
Without one, this interpolated vertex normal is the only normal the terrain has.

The sun's shading follows that normal.

### Hair: three Marschner lobes {#c9-hair kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {append baseLight {'
    {hairLight -25.0}
    {hairRoughness 0.65}
    {hairCuticleTilt 0.08}
    {hairLobes 0.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    {forwardStage 3.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 0.0 14.0 6.6} {pose 16.0 18.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {hairLobes 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
One more kind of surface has shading of its own, and that is hair.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairLight 0.0 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-pass-after-opaque"}}}
Hair, from the AtomTressFX gem, is drawn by passes of its own after the opaque frame, and composited over it.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairRoughness 0.75 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-default-model"}}}
Its default lighting is Marschner's model, which sums three lobes.

${cue "key" {' {hairLobes 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}
R reflects off the strand's surface, TT passes through the strand, and TRT reflects off its inside.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairLight 25.0 sentence}}}
The light is their sum, ${formulaSaid "R + TT + TRT" "R plus T T plus T R T" {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairRoughness 0.9 sentence}}}
${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-longitudinal"}}}
Each lobe is a Gaussian along the strand, times a second term for the angle around the strand.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairRoughness 0.65 sentence}}}
The model uses a refractive index of one point five five.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hair-cuticle-default"} {glide hairCuticleTilt 0.2 sentence}}}
It also tilts the cuticle, the strand's scaly surface, by zero point zero eight radians, the engine's choice.

${cue "key" {' {glide hairCuticleTilt 0.08 sentence}}}
The lab has no strands, so it draws the three lobes beside the sphere as an illustration.

### Special geometry, the same frame {#c9-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTerrain panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {hairRoughness 0.65}
    {hairCuticleTilt 0.08}
    {hairLobes 0.0}
    {labels 0.0}
    viewModeFinal
    {terrainO3deNormal 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 28.0 22.0} {pose 6.0 22.0 24.0} {pose 36.0 32.0 20.0}}}
        {path {' sunYaw} {' {' 160.0} {' 100.0}} beat}}}}}

So terrain brings its own shader, but it writes the same targets and reads the same lights and shadows.

Hair lights itself in passes of its own, after the opaque frame.

All that is left is to follow one pixel back through every step it took.

